Thoughts: the calendar is right; “George go ham on the whole repo” as one job is not.
Waiting until the Saturday reset is sensible. You’re in the middle of real data and real bugs. Burning the last 60% of the week on a refactor means you can’t finish the load or capture the next wave of issues. Use Wed–Sat for:

Data in
Bugs only if they block you
A written hit list of temp tools and known smells (no coding the cleanup yet)

Token reset Sat 11:00 gives you a clean window for a controlled 0.950 pass, then a short “did we break the ledger?” week before 1.0.
Sequence I’d use after reset

Backup + git tag (data + code).
Remove temp tools first (Bank Export, Beancount mass import, bulk manager, single Import-from-Text if you’re retiring it). Separate prompts, one family at a time, smoke test after each.
Then optimization — not one “rewrite everything” prompt.

A single “analyze the entire codebase and optimize” run tends to:

Touch too many files at once
“Clean up” working patterns you rely on
Mix style nits with real bugs
Make the second beta pass un-diagnosable

Better 0.950 shape (still “George + 4.6 High”)
Pass A – map, don’t change

Read-only: inventory duplication, dead code, leftover TEMP flags, fat ledger.php, repeated JS, query hot spots. Deliver a written list with severity.
Pass B – safe mechanical cleanup

Dead code, unused includes, obvious duplication, consistent helpers. No behavior change.
Pass C – targeted performance

Only what the map flagged (ledger list queries, filter unique-value loads, dashboard totals). Measure-ish, don’t guess.
Pass D – second beta

You click the same paths you use every day: add/edit, filters, attachments, backup/restore, dashboard cash, fund balances. If something broke, you know it came from B or C.
Features vs 0.950
Park new squirrels after Friday unless they block the books. Optimization week is not when you add dashboard builders or generic importers. Those are 1.0+ or a freeze exception you choose on purpose.
Bottom line
Yes: data + bugs until reset → strip temp tools → structured 0.950 review → short re-test → 1.0.

No: one unbounded “optimize the whole app” prompt.
If you want, before Saturday I can turn this into a one-page 0.950 runbook (prompt A/B/C text stubs + temp-tool removal order) so you’re not inventing it at 11:01.
